<h2>Chapter 4: On-Page Local SEO</h2>
<p>Your website is the foundation that supports everything else. A perfectly optimized Google Business Profile sends traffic to a weak website, and that traffic bounces. A strong website reinforces your GBP signals and captures organic traffic the Local Pack misses.</p>
<p>This chapter covers the on-page elements that matter most for service-area businesses.</p>
<h3>Location Page Architecture</h3>
<p>Location pages are dedicated pages on your website targeting "[service] + [city]" searches. They are the #1 organic local ranking factor and one of the highest-ROI content investments you can make.</p>
<p><strong>The location page formula:</strong></p>
<div class="code-block">
<pre><code>URL: /locations/[city-name]/
H1: [Primary Service] in [City], [State]
Word count: 800-1,500 words
</code></pre>
</div>
<p><strong>Required elements for every location page:</strong></p>
<div class="cost-table">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Element</th>
<th>Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Unique H1</strong></td>
<td>"Plumbing Services in Dallas, TX" — not "Dallas Plumbing"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Intro paragraph</strong></td>
<td>100-150 words about serving this specific city</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Service list</strong></td>
<td>Link to each service you offer in this city</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Local proof</strong></td>
<td>Projects completed, neighborhoods served, local landmarks</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Customer testimonial</strong></td>
<td>Review from a customer in this city</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Embedded map</strong></td>
<td>Google Map showing your service area</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>NAP block</strong></td>
<td>Name, address, phone — matching GBP exactly</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Local schema</strong></td>
<td>LocalBusiness + Service markup</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>CTA</strong></td>
<td>Click-to-call phone number, booking form</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p><strong>The thin content trap:</strong> Simply swapping city names on a template page triggers Google's duplicate content penalty. Each location page must have genuinely unique content. Here is how to make them unique:</p>
<ul class="bullet-list">
<li><strong>Mention specific neighborhoods</strong> you serve within that city.</li>
<li><strong>Reference local landmarks</strong> or events ("We have been serving the Oak Lawn neighborhood near Turtle Creek for 15 years").</li>
<li><strong>Include a unique customer story</strong> or testimonial from that city.</li>
<li><strong>Describe city-specific challenges</strong> ("Dallas homes built before 1980 often have galvanized steel pipes that corrode").</li>
<li><strong>Show photos</strong> from actual projects in that city.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Service area page strategy:</strong> If you serve 15 cities, create location pages for your top 5-7 cities. For smaller surrounding towns, create service area pages that group 2-3 nearby communities together.</p>
<h3>Service Page Local Optimization</h3>
<p>Every service you offer needs its own dedicated page. A single "Services" page with a bulleted list is not enough.</p>
<p><strong>Service page structure:</strong></p>
<div class="code-block">
<pre><code>URL: /services/[service-name]/
H1: [Service Name] in [Primary City] | [Business Name]
Word count: 1,000-2,000 words
</code></pre>
</div>
<p><strong>Service page sections:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>What this service is</strong> — Direct answer for voice search and AI Overviews.</li>
<li><strong>Who needs it</strong> — Qualify the reader and build relevance.</li>
<li><strong>Your process</strong> — Step-by-step breakdown of how you deliver the service.</li>
<li><strong>Why choose you</strong> — Differentiators, credentials, guarantees.</li>
<li><strong>Pricing information</strong> — Even a range builds trust and captures price-comparison searches.</li>
<li><strong>FAQs</strong> — 5-10 common questions with concise answers.</li>
<li><strong>Related services</strong> — Internal links to complementary services.</li>
<li><strong>CTA</strong> — Phone number, form, or booking button.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Local optimization for service pages:</strong><br />
- Include your primary city naturally 3-5 times in the content.<br />
- Mention 2-3 surrounding cities in the "Areas We Serve" section.<br />
- Add a "Recent [Service] Projects in [City]" section with photos.<br />
- Include LocalBusiness schema with service area markup.</p>
<h3>NAP Consistency</h3>
<p>NAP stands for Name, Address, Phone number. Consistency across every online mention of your business is critical for local SEO.</p>
<p><strong>The NAP consistency rule:</strong> Your business name, address, and phone number must match character-for-character across your website, Google Business Profile, and every directory listing.</p>
<p><strong>Common NAP inconsistencies that destroy rankings:</strong></p>
<div class="cost-table">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Inconsistent Element</th>
<th>Example Problem</th>
<th>Correct Approach</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Business name</strong></td>
<td>"Johnson Plumbing" vs. "Johnson Plumbing LLC" vs. "Johnson Plumbing Inc."</td>
<td>Pick one legal name and use it everywhere</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Street address</strong></td>
<td>"123 Main St" vs. "123 Main Street" vs. "123 N Main St"</td>
<td>Choose one format and stick to it</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Suite/unit</strong></td>
<td>"Suite 100" vs. "Ste 100" vs. "#100"</td>
<td>Standardize abbreviations</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Phone number</strong></td>
<td>"(214) 555-0199" vs. "214-555-0199" vs. "214.555.0199"</td>
<td>Use parentheses and dashes consistently</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Website URL</strong></td>
<td>"https://" vs. "http://" vs. trailing slash variations</td>
<td>Choose one and redirect all others</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p><strong>Create a "Single Source of Truth" document:</strong></p>
<div class="code-block">
<pre><code>Business Name: [Exact legal name]
Address Line 1: [Exact street address]
Address Line 2: [Suite/unit if applicable]
City: [City name]
State: [Two-letter abbreviation]
ZIP: [Full ZIP code]
Phone: [(XXX) XXX-XXXX]
Website: [Exact URL]
</code></pre>
</div>
<p>Copy and paste from this document every time you create a new listing. Never retype your NAP from memory.</p>
<h3>Schema Markup for Local Businesses</h3>
<p>Schema markup is structured data code that helps search engines understand your business information. For local SEO, it is essential.</p>
<p><strong>The three schema types every contractor needs:</strong></p>
<p><strong>1. LocalBusiness schema (homepage and location pages):</strong></p>
<div class="code-block">
<pre><code class="language-json">{
  &quot;@context&quot;: &quot;https://schema.org&quot;,
  &quot;@type&quot;: &quot;Plumber&quot;,
  &quot;@id&quot;: &quot;https://yoursite.com/#business&quot;,
  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;ClearFlow Plumbing&quot;,
  &quot;url&quot;: &quot;https://yoursite.com&quot;,
  &quot;telephone&quot;: &quot;+1-214-555-0199&quot;,
  &quot;image&quot;: &quot;https://yoursite.com/logo.png&quot;,
  &quot;priceRange&quot;: &quot;$$&quot;,
  &quot;address&quot;: {
    &quot;@type&quot;: &quot;PostalAddress&quot;,
    &quot;streetAddress&quot;: &quot;123 Main St&quot;,
    &quot;addressLocality&quot;: &quot;Dallas&quot;,
    &quot;addressRegion&quot;: &quot;TX&quot;,
    &quot;postalCode&quot;: &quot;75201&quot;,
    &quot;addressCountry&quot;: &quot;US&quot;
  },
  &quot;geo&quot;: {
    &quot;@type&quot;: &quot;GeoCoordinates&quot;,
    &quot;latitude&quot;: 32.77900,
    &quot;longitude&quot;: -96.80000
  },
  &quot;areaServed&quot;: [&quot;Dallas&quot;, &quot;Plano&quot;, &quot;Richardson&quot;, &quot;Frisco&quot;],
  &quot;openingHours&quot;: [&quot;Mo-Fr 08:00-18:00&quot;, &quot;Sa 09:00-13:00&quot;],
  &quot;sameAs&quot;: [
    &quot;https://maps.google.com/?cid=1234567890&quot;,
    &quot;https://www.facebook.com/clearflowplumbing&quot;
  ]
}
</code></pre>
</div>
<p><strong>2. Service schema (service pages):</strong></p>
<div class="code-block">
<pre><code class="language-json">{
  &quot;@context&quot;: &quot;https://schema.org&quot;,
  &quot;@type&quot;: &quot;Service&quot;,
  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Emergency Drain Cleaning&quot;,
  &quot;provider&quot;: {
    &quot;@type&quot;: &quot;Plumber&quot;,
    &quot;name&quot;: &quot;ClearFlow Plumbing&quot;
  },
  &quot;areaServed&quot;: {
    &quot;@type&quot;: &quot;City&quot;,
    &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Dallas&quot;
  },
  &quot;description&quot;: &quot;24/7 emergency drain cleaning services in Dallas and surrounding areas.&quot;
}
</code></pre>
</div>
<p><strong>3. FAQ schema (service pages and FAQ pages):</strong></p>
<div class="code-block">
<pre><code class="language-json">{
  &quot;@context&quot;: &quot;https://schema.org&quot;,
  &quot;@type&quot;: &quot;FAQPage&quot;,
  &quot;mainEntity&quot;: [{
    &quot;@type&quot;: &quot;Question&quot;,
    &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Do you offer emergency plumbing in Dallas?&quot;,
    &quot;acceptedAnswer&quot;: {
      &quot;@type&quot;: &quot;Answer&quot;,
      &quot;text&quot;: &quot;Yes, ClearFlow Plumbing provides 24/7 emergency plumbing services throughout Dallas, Plano, and Frisco. Our technicians typically arrive within 60 minutes.&quot;
    }
  }]
}
</code></pre>
</div>
<p><strong>Schema implementation tips:</strong><br />
- Use JSON-LD format — Google's preferred method.<br />
- Include <code>@id</code> references to connect entities across your site.<br />
- Match schema data exactly to visible page content.<br />
- Validate with Google's Rich Results Test tool.<br />
- Update schema whenever business information changes.</p>
