<h2>Chapter 6: Multi-Location Local SEO</h2>
<p>If you operate multiple offices or serve multiple major markets, your local SEO strategy scales in complexity. One website with dedicated location pages outperforms separate websites for each location. This chapter covers how to manage local SEO across multiple service areas without diluting your authority.</p>
<h3>Location Page Strategy</h3>
<p>Each physical location needs its own dedicated landing page. These pages should not be copies of each other with swapped city names.</p>
<p><strong>Multi-location page structure:</strong></p>
<div class="code-block">
<pre><code>Homepage
  └── Locations
        ├── Dallas Office
        │     ├── Services in Dallas
        │     ├── Projects in Dallas
        │     └── Dallas Team
        ├── Plano Office
        │     ├── Services in Plano
        │     ├── Projects in Plano
        │     └── Plano Team
        └── Frisco Office
              ├── Services in Frisco
              ├── Projects in Frisco
              └── Frisco Team
</code></pre>
</div>
<p><strong>URL structure best practices:</strong></p>
<div class="cost-table">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Approach</th>
<th>Example</th>
<th>Best For</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Subdirectory</strong></td>
<td><code>/locations/dallas/</code></td>
<td>Most businesses — consolidates authority</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Subdomain</strong></td>
<td><code>dallas.yoursite.com</code></td>
<td>Very large franchises — technical complexity</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Separate domains</strong></td>
<td><code>dallasplumbing.com</code></td>
<td>Not recommended — splits authority</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p><strong>The subdirectory approach (<code>/locations/city/</code>) is best for 99% of multi-location home service businesses.</strong> It keeps all authority on one domain while allowing Google to understand your geographic coverage.</p>
<p><strong>Unique content requirements per location page:</strong><br />
- Different team member photos and bios.<br />
- Different customer testimonials.<br />
- Different project photos from that city.<br />
- Different local landmarks or neighborhood mentions.<br />
- Different service area maps.<br />
- Unique phone number (if available) or click-to-call tracking number.</p>
<h3>GBP Management for Multiple Locations</h3>
<p>Each physical location needs its own verified Google Business Profile. Never try to manage multiple addresses from one profile.</p>
<p><strong>Multi-location GBP setup:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Create separate profiles</strong> for each location with unique addresses.</li>
<li><strong>Use unique phone numbers</strong> if possible (local numbers outperform toll-free).</li>
<li><strong>Link each profile to its location page</strong> (<code>/locations/dallas/</code>), not your homepage.</li>
<li><strong>Use Google Business Profile Manager</strong> for bulk management if you have 10+ locations.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>GBP Manager benefits:</strong><br />
- Manage all locations from one dashboard.<br />
- Bulk upload photos, posts, and updates.<br />
- Compare performance across locations.<br />
- Delegate management to location managers.</p>
<p><strong>Review management at scale:</strong><br />
- Assign a local manager or technician to respond to reviews for their location.<br />
- Use a reputation management tool to monitor all locations centrally.<br />
- Set response time goals (within 48 hours for all reviews).</p>
